missile-defense-trainer for Debian
===================================

Getting PyTorch, which this package deliberately does not depend on
-------------------------------------------------------------------

Training needs PyTorch. `python3-torch` is only Suggested, for two reasons that
both point the same way: Debian's copy is CPU-only and lags upstream, and the
vendor builds that people actually train on — CUDA, ROCm — are multi-gigabyte
wheels published outside the archive. A hard dependency would therefore install
a second copy that no real run uses.

So the absence has to be arranged for rather than assumed away. There are two
supported ways, and the first is the one to reach for.

1. Let the trainer do it (recommended)
--------------------------------------

Open the trainer:

    missile-defense-trainer

If this machine has no PyTorch, the Start button is disabled with the reason
next to it, and the trainer offers to install a training runtime. It builds a
virtualenv under the per-user data directory, picks the right build for the
hardware it finds — CUDA, ROCm or CPU — and health-checks the result by
importing both torch and the simulation binding before it becomes the live one.
Nothing is mutated in place, so a failed or cancelled install cannot damage a
working runtime, and nothing is installed from anywhere except pypi.org or
download.pytorch.org.

This needs `python3-venv`, which this package depends on, and a network
connection. A CUDA runtime is roughly 4.5 GB.

2. A virtualenv you make yourself
---------------------------------

Debian 12 and later mark the system interpreter *externally managed* (PEP 668),
so `pip install torch` into it fails by design — correctly, and it is not a
thing to work around with --break-system-packages. Use a virtualenv:

    python3 -m venv --system-site-packages ~/.venvs/md
    ~/.venvs/md/bin/pip install torch
    ~/.venvs/md/bin/missile-defense-train

`--system-site-packages` is the whole trick: the venv sees the packaged
`_md_native` extension and the `md` package from python3-md, while pip owns
torch inside the venv and touches nothing the archive installed.

For a GPU build, take the index URL from https://pytorch.org/get-started/ —
for example:

    ~/.venvs/md/bin/pip install torch --index-url \
        https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu130

The CUDA build must not be newer than the maximum CUDA version your driver
reports in `nvidia-smi`.

Where things are written
------------------------

Runs, recordings, exported policies and any managed runtime go under
`$XDG_DATA_HOME/MissileDefense` (`~/.local/share/MissileDefense` by default).
Nothing is written outside the user's home directory, and the packages install
no runtime data of their own.

The game is a separate package
------------------------------

`missile-defense` is Recommended, not Depended on: the trainer can start,
browse runs and compare policies with no game installed. It is what opens a
recording when you double-click one, so install it if you want to watch what
your agent did rather than only read the curve.

